{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 31, "model_name": "mistralai/mixtral-8x7b-instruct-v0.1", "content": " Raymond III Count of Tripoli also known as Raymond-Rupen was a nobleman of the late 12th century who held the titles of Count of Tripoli and Prince of Antioch. He was born in 1140 and died in 1187.\n\n\nRaymond III was the only son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. In 1152 he married Eschiva of Saint Omer, daughter of William of Saint Omer, Lord of Transjordan, and Agnes of Courtenay. Together they had two children: Bohemond III and Bohemond IV.\n\n\nRaymond III became Count of Tripoli in 1152 after his father's death. He was a vassal of the Kingdom of Jerusalem, which was ruled by his uncle, King Baldwin III. In 1153, Raymond III was present at the Siege of Ascalon, the last major military campaign of the First Crusade. He also participated in the Battle of Harim in 1164, where he was captured by the Zengids and imprisoned for 16 years.\n\n\n*Highlighted section part 1: Prince of Antioch*\n\n\nIn 1169, Raymond III became Prince of Antioch by default, as the previous prince, Bohemond III, was a minor. He served as regent for Bohemond III and ruled Antioch until 1174, when Bohemond III came of age. During his tenure as prince, Raymond III faced a number of challenges, including the threat of invasion by the Byzantine Empire and the Zengids. In 1172, he was able to negotiate a peace treaty with the Byzantine Emperor Manuel I Comnenus, but he was less successful in his dealings with the Zengids. In 1174, the Zengid sultan Nur al-Din launched an invasion of Antioch, and Raymond III was forced to pay a large tribute in order to preserve his rule.\n\n\n*Highlighted section part 2: Battle of Hattin*\n\n\nRaymond III's most significant military engagement was the Battle of Hattin in 1187, where he fought on the side of the Kingdom of Jerusalem against the Ayyubids under the command of Saladin. The battle was a disaster for the Christians, and Raymond III was one of the few noblemen to survive. He was captured by the Ayyubids and held prisoner until 1190, when he was ransomed by his son Bohemond III.\n\n\n*Highlighted section part 3: Death*\n\n\nRaymond III died in 1190 while still in captivity. He was succeeded as Count of Tripoli by his son Bohemond III. Raymond III's legacy was mixed. On the one hand, he was a skilled diplomat and administrator who was able to maintain his rule in the face of significant challenges. On the other hand, he was a controversial figure who was accused of cowardice and treachery by some of his contemporaries. Despite these controversies, however, Raymond III is remembered as an important figure in the history of the Crusader states in the Middle East.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -3.9219088648678735e-05}, {"token": "III", "logprob": -0.0001627074379939586}, {"token": "Count", "logprob": -0.3794739544391632}, {"token": "of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "Tri", "logprob": -8.940656698541716e-06}, {"token": "pol", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": "i", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "also", "logprob": -0.515670657157898}, {"token": "known", "logprob": -0.0033385988790541887}, {"token": "as", "logprob": -2.3841855067985307e-07}, {"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -1.549708758830093e-05}, {"token": "-", "logprob": -4.076565742492676}, {"token": "R", "logprob": -3.516612196108326e-05}, {"token": "up", "logprob": -0.8842123746871948}, {"token": "en", "logprob": -2.7418097943154862e-06}, {"token": "was", "logprob": -0.019012007862329483}, {"token": "a", "logprob": -0.29543450474739075}, {"token": "noble", "logprob": -0.1830405741930008}, {"token": "man", "logprob": -0.018931066617369652}, {"token": "of", "logprob": -0.5596039891242981}, {"token": "the", "logprob": -0.6340491771697998}, {"token": "late", "logprob": -1.0236320495605469}, {"token": "", "logprob": -0.05651786923408508}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -2.3841855067985307e-07}, {"token"